/-! # A reduced Hopf core with nilpotent augmentation fibre

Source: Milne, Algebraic Groups (2017), Theorem 3.23, Proposition 3.29 and
Theorem 3.31, printed pp. 71--74.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-hopf-reduced-core-nilpotent-fibre)
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u v

namespace HopfAlgebra

/-- A finite type commutative Hopf algebra over an algebraically closed field contains
an affine reduced Hopf core with nilpotent augmentation fibre. The bialgebra map `i`
is required to be the inclusion on every element and to preserve the antipode.
The quotient ideal is the ideal generated in `A` by the counit-zero elements of `C`.
The equation with `Ideal.Quotient.mkₐ` characterizes the induced counit uniquely.

Source: Milne, Algebraic Groups (2017), Theorem 3.23, Proposition 3.29 and
Theorem 3.31, printed pp. 71--74.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-hopf-reduced-core-nilpotent-fibre)

Proof plan:
1. Finite type over a field implies Noetherian, by
   lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-algebra-noetherian. Choose algebra generators `x₁, …, xₘ`.
2. In characteristic zero, a nonzero nilpotent `b` has a proper annihilator contained
   in a maximal ideal, by lem-weil-pbc-proper-ideal-contained-in-maximal.
   By lem-wf-affine-nullstellensatz-vanishing (1) this ideal is the kernel of a
   rational point `g`. The local Cartier statement
   lem-sfb-hopf-nilpotent-rational-annihilator supplies `t` with `g(t) = 1` and
   `t * b = 0`, a contradiction. Take `C = A`; the quotient counit is injective,
   so its kernel is zero and exponent one works.
3. In positive characteristic `p`, choose generators `zᵢ` of the nilradical and
   exponents `eᵢ ≥ 1` killing them. Its power `n = 1 + ∑ (eᵢ - 1)` vanishes:
   every monomial of degree `n` contains one of the powers `zᵢ ^ eᵢ`.
   Choose `r ≥ 1` with `q = p ^ r ≥ n`.
4. By def-weil-ring-frobenius the `q`th power map is a ring map. Algebraic closure
   gives `q`th roots of all scalars, so its image `C` is a subalgebra. It is generated
   by the `xᵢ ^ q`. If `a ^ q` is nilpotent then `a` belongs to the nilradical,
   hence `a ^ q = 0`, proving that `C` is reduced.
5. Tensor powers of the inclusion of vector spaces are injective. The Frobenius
   identity gives `Δ(a ^ q) = ∑ uᵢ ^ q ⊗ vᵢ ^ q`; the antipode also preserves
   `q`th powers. Restrict the structure maps, and check all Hopf identities after
   the injective inclusions into the tensor powers of `A`.
6. Put `yᵢ = xᵢ - ε(xᵢ)`. Polynomial expansion gives `ker ε = (y₁, …, yₘ)`.
   The ideal generated by the counit-zero elements of `C` lies in `ker ε`, giving
   the quotient counit. Its kernel is generated by the classes of the `yᵢ`, whose
   `q`th powers vanish because `yᵢ ^ q` lies in `C⁺`. Degree `1 + m * (q - 1)`
   monomials vanish, proving the claimed nilpotence. Empty generating families
   give exponent one in both nilpotence calculations. -/
theorem exists_reduced_core_nilpotent_fibre
    (k : Type u) (A : Type v) [Field k] [IsAlgClosed k] [CommRing A]
    [HopfAlgebra k A] (hA : (algebraMap k A).FiniteType) :
    ∃ C : Subalgebra k A,
      (algebraMap k C).FiniteType ∧ IsReduced C ∧
      (∃ hC : HopfAlgebra k C,
        hC.toBialgebra.toAlgebra = (inferInstance : Algebra k C) ∧
        ∃ i : @BialgHom k C A _ _ hC.toBialgebra.toAlgebra _ _
            hC.toBialgebra.toCoalgebra.toCoalgebraStruct _,
        (∀ c : C, i c = (c : A)) ∧
        ∀ c : C,
          i (@antipode k C _ _ hC.toHopfAlgebraStruct c) = antipode k (i c)) ∧
      (∃ εbar :
          (A ⧸ Ideal.span {a : A | a ∈ C ∧ Bialgebra.counitAlgHom k A a = 0}) →ₐ[k] k,
        εbar.comp (Ideal.Quotient.mkₐ k
          (Ideal.span {a : A | a ∈ C ∧ Bialgebra.counitAlgHom k A a = 0})) =
            Bialgebra.counitAlgHom k A ∧
        ∃ N : ℕ, 1 ≤ N ∧ RingHom.ker εbar ^ N = ⊥) ∧
      (CharZero k → C = ⊤) ∧
      ∀ p : ℕ, p.Prime → (p : k) = 0 →
        ∃ r : ℕ, 1 ≤ r ∧ (C : Set A) = Set.range (fun a : A ↦ a ^ (p ^ r)) := by
  sorry

end HopfAlgebra
